@lesson gpus
Q: Batch-1 decoding of a 7B model in bf16 on a GPU with 3 TB/s of memory bandwidth: what is the best-case tokens per second? (nearest 10)
= 214 ±15
> Each token reads all 14 GB of weights: 3000 GB/s ÷ 14 GB ≈ 214 tokens/s at most.

Q: Which operations are typically memory-bound? (select all)
* Elementwise additions and activations
* Layer normalization and softmax
* Small-batch LLM decoding
- Large matrix multiplications
> Large matmuls do many FLOPs per byte and are compute-bound.

Q: What does kernel fusion improve?
* It reduces memory traffic by doing several operations on data while it is on-chip
- It increases model accuracy
- It adds more GPU cores
- It reduces the number of parameters
> FlashAttention and torch.compile both rely on fusion.

Q: What is MFU?
* Achieved useful model FLOP/s divided by the hardware's peak FLOP/s
- Memory free on the GPU
- The number of GPUs used
- Mean forward latency
> Good large-scale training runs reach roughly 40–60%.

@lesson distributed
Q: In data parallelism, what is communicated between GPUs every step?
- The training data
* Gradients, averaged with an all-reduce
- The model architecture
- Nothing
> Each GPU holds a full model copy and processes a different slice of the batch.

Q: Which ZeRO stage also shards the model weights themselves (equivalent to FSDP)?
- Stage 1
- Stage 2
* Stage 3
- None
> Stage 1 shards optimizer states, stage 2 adds gradients, stage 3 adds weights.

Q: A pipeline has 4 stages and 12 micro-batches. What fraction of time is the bubble, \((p-1)/(m+p-1)\)? (three decimals)
= 0.2 ±0.002
> \(3/15=0.2\). More micro-batches shrink the bubble.

Q: Why is tensor parallelism usually kept within a single node?
* It needs very fast links because activations are exchanged every layer
- It cannot use GPUs
- It needs no communication
- It only works for CNNs
> NVLink-class bandwidth inside a node makes per-layer collectives affordable.

@lesson ml-system-design
Q: What should come first in an ML system design answer?
* Clarifying the goal, users, scale and constraints
- Choosing the neural architecture
- Writing code
- Picking a cloud provider
> The design depends on latency, scale and what success means.

Q: 100 million daily users make 20 requests each. Roughly what is the average QPS? (nearest thousand)
= 23000 ±1000
> \(2\times10^9/86{,}400\approx23{,}000\) requests per second; plan for peaks of 2–3×.

Q: Which signals indicate seniority in a design interview? (select all)
* Starting with a simple baseline before a complex model
* Explicit trade-offs between latency, quality and cost
* A plan for monitoring and iteration after launch
- Naming the biggest model available
> Interviewers listen for judgement, not buzzwords.

@lesson case-studies
Q: For payment fraud detection, why choose the threshold by expected cost?
* Missed fraud and blocked good customers have very different costs
- Thresholds should always be 0.5
- To maximize accuracy
- Because AUC requires it
> Expected cost (and review capacity) define the right operating point.

Q: In feed recommendation, why optimize for long-term satisfaction rather than clicks alone?
* Click optimization rewards clickbait and can hurt retention
- Clicks cannot be measured
- Satisfaction is easier to compute
- Clicks are always noise
> Multi-objective ranking balances engagement with quality signals.

Q: In content moderation, recall targets should be higher for the most severe harm categories.
= true
> Missing severe content is far costlier than extra review for less severe categories.

@lesson exam
Q: Inference for a 13B model is too slow at batch size 1. Which changes raise throughput the most? (select all)
* Continuous batching of concurrent requests
* Weight quantization
* Speculative decoding
- Adding more CPU RAM
> Decoding is memory-bound; batching and fewer bytes per weight help directly.

Q: A 7B model needs about 112 GB for training state with Adam. With ZeRO-3 across 8 GPUs, roughly how many GB per GPU (before activations)?
= 14 ±1
> 112 / 8 = 14 GB when weights, gradients and optimizer states are all sharded.

Q: What is the arithmetic intensity of adding two fp16 vectors (1 FLOP per element, 6 bytes moved)? (two decimals)
= 0.17 ±0.01
> 1/6 ≈ 0.17 FLOP per byte: firmly memory-bound.

Q: In a recommendation system design, where should the heavy model with rich cross features run?
* In the ranking stage, on a few hundred candidates
- On every item in the catalogue
- In the data pipeline only
- Nowhere
> Candidate generation narrows the set first.
